    "blogtextliquid_ra7Awx": {
      "type": "blogtextliquid",
      "custom_css": [],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>Dry skin is the single biggest culprit behind poor fragrance longevity. Scent molecules are lipid-soluble—they need moisture and oils to cling to. When you apply perfume to parched, unmoisturized skin, your skin literally drinks the alcohol and evaporates the fragrance oils within two hours.</p><p>A simple 12-second routine before you spray can double or triple the wear time of any fragrance in your collection. Here is the science behind locking down your scent from morning to midnight.</p>"
      }
    },
    "blocks_ji7beH": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_614820f_zNd6WG": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_614820f",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Pulse Point Placement vs Clothing Application",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_size_mobile": 28,
            "heading_line_height": 1.1,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_margin_bottom": 30,
            "heading_margin_bottom_mobile": 25,
            "column_1_text": "<p>Pulse points generate radiant body heat: the sides of the neck, behind the ears, the wrists, and the inner crooks of the elbows. Heat expands fragrance molecules, projecting them outward into the air around you.</p><p>However, high heat also accelerates evaporation. If your pulse points are dry, the top and middle notes burn off rapidly, leaving only faint base notes behind by mid-afternoon.</p>",
            "column_2_text": "<p>Spraying your clothing works on an entirely different physical principle. Textile fibers (especially wool, cashmere, and cotton) do not have body heat or natural skin oils. They trap fragrance molecules deep within the fabric weave.</p><p>While clothing application does not project as dynamically as skin, it preserves the true fragrance profile for 24 to 48 hours without changing.</p>",
            "body_size": 17,
            "body_size_mobile": 15,
            "body_line_height": 1.9,
            "body_color": "#2D2A26",
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "column_gap": 55,
            "column_gap_mobile": 30,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "padding_top": 60,
            "padding_bottom": 60,
            "padding_horizontal": 40,
            "padding_top_mobile": 40,
            "padding_bottom_mobile": 40,
            "padding_horizontal_mobile": 20
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "ai_gen_block_614820f_zNd6WG"
      ],
      "name": "Editorial text section",
      "settings": {}
    },
    "1778690749e24fd023": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_fb81b45_9nTtAA": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_fb81b45",
          "settings": {
            "desktop_width_percent": 100,
            "max_width": 1100,
            "section_padding": 60,
            "background_color": "#FAF8F5",
            "heading": "<p>The 12-second moisturizer and primer routine</p>",
            "heading_size": 42,
            "heading_color": "#1A1816",
            "heading_spacing": 25,
            "box_color": "#1C1917",
            "box_radius": 15,
            "box_padding": 45,
            "box_spacing": 35,
            "box_paragraph_1": "<p>The single most effective longevity hack in perfumery is applying an unscented lotion or a tiny dab of petroleum jelly (Vaseline) to your pulse points before spraying. The occlusive barrier creates a lipid foundation that binds to the perfume oils, slowing down evaporation by over 60%.</p><p>Make sure the moisturizer is 100% fragrance-free so it does not compete with or alter the delicate aroma compounds of your perfume.</p><p>Combine this technique with an Extrait or Eau de Parfum formulation like <strong>Fierce</strong> or <strong>Addiction</strong>, and you will easily achieve 10+ hours of continuous, balanced projection.</p>",
            "box_paragraph_2": "",
            "box_paragraph_3": "",
            "box_text_color": "#F5EFE6",
            "box_text_size": 17,
            "box_text_line_height": 1.9,
            "paragraph_spacing": 20,
            "subheading": "<p>Understanding nose blindness (olfactory fatigue)</p>",
            "subheading_size": 42,
            "subheading_color": "#1A1816",
            "subheading_spacing": 40,
            "bottom_text": "<p>Before you reapply a third time, consider whether the scent is actually gone or if your brain has simply tuned it out. The human olfactory bulb is hardwired to ignore constant sensory stimuli after twenty minutes to prevent sensory overload.</p><p>Just because you can no longer smell your perfume does not mean the people around you cannot. If in doubt, ask a friend before spraying again!</p>",
            "bottom_text_size": 17,
            "bottom_text_color": "#2D2A26",
            "bottom_text_line_height": 1.9
          },
          "blocks": {}
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "ai_gen_block_fb81b45_9nTtAA"
      ],
      "settings": {}
    },
